--- body ---
## Context

During the 2026-06-03 raid (merge requests #238–#242), the /verify
pipeline produced correct final verdicts but its sub-skills misbehaved
four times in five runs:

1. `verify-gate` returned an unrelated tickets-backlog survey instead
   of gating #241 — the running verifier rendered the gate verdict
   itself from collected evidence (authorized fallback, but the gate
   did the wrong job silently).
2. `verify-adherence` spiraled into ticket-landscape orientation
   instead of checking #238's diff.
3. `verify-adherence` reviewed the wrong branch (t0189) during #240's
   run.
4. A verify sub-agent resurrected the historical duplicate-ID file
   `0149-squash-merge-probe-false-negatives.erg` into the raid
   orchestrator's worktree; `erg-pr-merge`'s `git add tickets/` swept
   it into the index and bounced #242's merge on corpus validation.

Common shape: sub-skill invocations lose their inputs (PR number,
branch, unresolved-findings list) when forked, and sub-agents inherit
the orchestrator's cwd instead of the review worktree. Memory
`feedback_verify_agents_dirty_main_repo` documents the main-repo
variant of (4); this raid shows it generalizes to orchestrator
worktrees.

## Diagnosis (2026-06-04, transcripts of session 5a6bf32d)

Subagent transcripts under
`~/.claude/projects/-home-haduong--claude--claude-worktrees-explore-tickets/5a6bf32d-*/subagents/`
pin the mechanism. Three findings correct the original hypothesis:

1. **Args are NOT lost.** Every fork received its argument correctly
   interpolated into the SKILL.md heading (`# Verify adherence —
   t0186-multi-ticket-close`, `# Verify gate — PR 241`). The verify
   orchestrator passed `{"skill": "verify-adherence", "args":
   "t0187-release-skill"}` etc. every time. What the fork loses is the
   *directive framing*: a `context: fork` sub-skill starts with a bare
   context — no parent conversation — whose only user message is the
   doc-style SKILL.md body. 4 of ~14 forks classified it as "skill
   documentation loaded, no explicit task" (verbatim in
   `agent-a789cbc448`, the rogue gate-241) and fell back to ambient
   cues. Drift is stochastic: the well-behaved gate-238 fork
   (`agent-a17d062f`) received byte-identical context shape. So a more
   imperative prompt lowers frequency but cannot be the safety
   mechanism.
2. **The ambient cues all point at the same wrong task.** Forks
   inherit (a) the session worktree's name `explore-tickets`, (b) the
   parent session-start gitStatus snapshot (stale branch
   `idh-0187-release-skill`), (c) the shared task list. All four
   drifted forks converged on the same hallucinated task: survey the
   tickets backlog. The PR-#243 incident is the worst case: the fork's
   cwd was the *session* worktree — not the orchestrator's
   `/tmp/review-<pr>` worktree, which a forked sub-skill does not
   inherit — and the raid session had left that worktree sitting on
   local branch `t0189-followup` (created 20:46Z to file ticket 0192).
   The drifted fork (`agent-a5156594`, 20:50Z) found committed work
   with no PR, inferred "open a PR" as its mission, consulted the
   advisor (which endorsed it), pushed the branch, and opened #243.
3. **The 0149 resurrection is a shared-stash hazard, not file
   creation.** The same rogue fork ran `git stash -u >/dev/null; …;
   git stash pop >/dev/null 2>&1 || true` on a clean tree: the stash
   saved nothing, so the pop silently popped a PRE-EXISTING stash from
   the repo-global stash stack (stashes are shared across all
   worktrees), surfacing the historical `0149-*.erg` as a DU conflict.
   The fork `git rm --cached`-ed it, leaving it untracked on disk
   ("harmless", it said); `erg-pr-merge`'s `git add tickets/` then
   swept it into #242's merge. The primary repo still holds 3 stale
   stashes as of 2026-06-04 — the hazard is live.

## Relevant files

- `skills/verify/SKILL.md` — orchestration; where sub-skill inputs are
  (not) threaded
- `skills/verify-adherence/SKILL.md` — drifted twice
- `skills/verify-gate/SKILL.md` — lost PR context once
- `skills/merge/erg-pr-merge` — `git add tickets/` sweeps stray files;
  consider `git add` of only the closed ticket paths

## Actions

Ranked by determinism — the containment fixes fire whether or not a
fork drifts; the prompt fix only lowers drift frequency.

1. **Pin sub-skill cwd via explicit path argument.** `context: fork`
   does not inherit the orchestrator's cwd, so verify/SKILL.md must
   pass the `/tmp/review-<pr>` worktree path as part of each
   sub-skill's args, and each sub-skill's SKILL.md must `cd` into the
   path it receives as its first act. (Deterministic.)
2. **Post-verify containment check.** Orchestrator-side `git status
   --porcelain` in both the session worktree and the primary repo
   after every /verify; discard foreign files and abort merge on
   unexpected branch switches. (Deterministic.)
3. **Narrow `erg-pr-merge`'s `git add tickets/`** to the exact paths
   `erg close`/`erg archive` touched, so stray files can never be
   swept into a merge. (Deterministic.)
4. **Ban bare `git stash`/`stash pop` in shared worktrees.** The stash
   stack is repo-global. Skills must not round-trip through it with
   output suppressed; use `git stash push` + pop-by-name, or
   `git worktree add` a throwaway instead. Also: drain the 3 stale
   stashes currently parked in the primary repo. (Deterministic.)
5. **Imperative task framing in fork-context sub-skills.** Open
   verify-adherence/verify-gate/review-pr SKILL.md with "Your task NOW:
   <verb> $ARGUMENTS" before the reference prose, so a bare fork cannot
   misread the body as loaded documentation. (Probabilistic — reduces
   drift frequency only; never rely on it for safety.)

## Test

Red step: grep-able assertions that (a) verify/SKILL.md threads the
review-worktree path into each sub-skill invocation line, (b) each
fork-context sub-skill SKILL.md opens with an imperative task line
referencing $ARGUMENTS, (c) erg-pr-merge stages only erg-touched
paths (fixture: stray tickets/NNNN file present, merge must not sweep
it).

## Verification

- [ ] sub-skill invocations carry the review-worktree path as an arg
- [ ] sub-skills cd into the received path before any git command
- [ ] post-verify status check present in skills/verify/SKILL.md
- [ ] erg-pr-merge stages only the closed-ticket paths
- [ ] no bare stash round-trip remains in skills/scripts

## Invariants

- /verify still never merges; verdict semantics unchanged.
- Existing guard and bash-suite tests stay green.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] One full raid cycle with zero off-task sub-skill runs and zero
      foreign files in the orchestrator worktree after verify
